# Islands

An island is a small Lustre app on a server-rendered page that runs in the
browser. The server renders it into the page like any other element, so it
shows at once and reads as plain HTML. Then the browser loads the same app,
compiled to JavaScript, and it takes over the elements already there.

A [live component](/docs/ui/live-components) runs on the server and sends
every click up a WebSocket. An island runs where the click happens, with no
connection and no round trip, so it suits things that change with every
keystroke or frame: a counter, a star rating, a filter over a list already
on the page, a drag handle, a timer. It cannot reach your database or your
services, because it is in the browser. When it needs data, it fetches it
like any other browser code, or the page hands it what it needs as it
renders.

Use a live component when the logic needs the server, and an island when
it does not. A page can have both, and an island can sit inside a live
view.

## A counter

An island is an ordinary Lustre app, in a module in a directory called
`islands`, such as `src/my_app/islands/`. The module has a function that
returns an `Island`:

```gleam
// src/my_app/islands/counter.gleam
import gleam/dynamic/decode
import gleam/int
import gleam/json
import howdy/ui/island.{type Island}
import lustre/element.{type Element, text}
import lustre/element/html
import lustre/event

pub fn island() -> Island(Int, Int, Msg) {
  island.simple(init:, update:, view:, flags: decode.int, encode: json.int)
}

pub type Msg {
  Increment
  Decrement
}

fn init(start: Int) -> Int {
  start
}

fn update(count: Int, msg: Msg) -> Int {
  case msg {
    Increment -> count + 1
    Decrement -> count - 1
  }
}

fn view(count: Int) -> Element(Msg) {
  html.div([], [
    html.button([event.on_click(Decrement)], [text("-")]),
    html.output([], [text(int.to_string(count))]),
    html.button([event.on_click(Increment)], [text("+")]),
  ])
}
```

`init` takes the island's *flags*: what the page hands it, here the count
to start from. The flags travel from the server to the browser as JSON, so
the island names how to write them, `encode`, and how to read them back,
`flags`. They can be any type the two can carry, such as a record of a
product's id, price and stock.

`island.simple` is for an island that only holds state, like
`lustre.simple`. `island.application` takes an `init` and `update` that
also return effects, like `lustre.application`. Effects from `init` run in
the browser only: the server renders the model `init` returns and ignores
the rest.

The app needs `lustre` and `gleam_json` in its own `gleam.toml`, since its
island modules import them.

## Mounting

Put the island in a [page](/docs/ui/pages) with `island.mount`, passing the
island's function itself, without calling it, and its flags:

```gleam
import howdy/ui/island
import my_app/islands/counter

page.new("Counter")
|> page.body([
  ui.container([], [
    ui.h1("Counter"),
    island.mount(counter.island, with: 5),
  ]),
])
|> page.respond(ctx)
```

The server calls `init(5)` and renders the view into the page. The browser
loads the module that `counter.island` lives in, decodes the flags and
starts the app on the rendered elements, keeping them rather than drawing
new ones. Nothing has to be registered: the function says which module to
load. Mount the same island as many times as you like, with the same flags
or different ones; each is its own app.

Every app with islands must also mount `ui.islands()`, which serves their
browser code under `/_howdy/islands`:

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(ui.islands())
|> howdy.controller(pages())
```

A page whose islands cannot load says so in a red banner, naming what to
check, rather than leaving dead buttons.

## What an island can use

Island modules, and every module of your app they import, are compiled for
JavaScript. They can use Gleam's standard library, Lustre, `gleam_json`
and anything else that runs in a browser, but not code that only runs on
the server, such as `howdy/database` or a module that calls Erlang. A
module shared by the server and an island, such as one that formats
prices, must run on both.

When an island imports something it cannot, the build fails with the
compiler's own error, naming the module and the line. In development it
shows on the page.

Only the island modules and what they import are compiled for JavaScript.
The rest of your app, in the same package, stays server-only and can use
anything.

## Development

In development, `ui.islands()` builds the islands whenever a page loads
them, under `gleam run` as well as `gleam dev`. Gleam skips the work when
nothing has changed, so a page load costs a few milliseconds, and the
browser always runs the code that is in your editor: change an island,
reload the page, and the change is there, with no restart.

A compile error in an island shows on the page, in a banner with the
compiler's message and the path to the file. Fix it and reload.

The islands are built in a hidden package, `build/howdy_islands`, which
links only the island modules and the app modules they import, with the
same dependency versions as the app. You never need to look at it.

## Components in islands

Howdy UI's [components](/docs/ui/components) work in islands too:

```gleam
fn view(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {
  card.card([], [
    card.header([], [card.title([text("Tasks")])]),
    card.content([], [
      input.input([
        attribute.value(model.draft),
        event.on_input(Typed),
      ]),
      button.button(button.Primary, [event.on_click(Added)], [text("Add")]),
    ]),
  ])
}
```

A class has the same name in the browser as on the server, so the rendered
island keeps its styles when the browser takes over. A class the browser
uses that the page did not, such as a badge that only appears after a
click, has its CSS added to the page as it first appears. Components whose
state the browser keeps, such as which tab is selected or whether a dialog
is open, keep it as the island re-renders.

Import components from their own modules, such as `howdy/ui/card`, rather
than through `howdy/ui`. Ids passed to components must still be unique in
the page, so an island mounted twice needs ids made from its flags.

## Islands in live views

An island can go in the view of a live component:

```gleam
fn view(model: Model) -> Element(Msg) {
  html.div([], [
    html.p([], [text("On the server: " <> int.to_string(model.count))]),
    island.mount(counter.island, with: 3),
  ])
}
```

The live view renders the island once and leaves it to the browser. The
island's events never reach the server, and the live view's re-renders
leave the island's state alone. If the live view renders a different
island in its place, or the same island with different flags, the old one
stops and the new one starts afresh from its flags.

## Timers and other running effects

An island removed from the page, such as by a live view re-rendering
without it, stops: its `update` and `view` no longer run. An effect waiting
to send it a message, such as a timeout, finds no one listening.

Effects that keep running, such as an interval or an event listener on the
window, would keep going, because Lustre gives effects no way to stop.
Start them with `island.every` or `island.subscription`, which stop them
with the island:

```gleam
fn init(milliseconds: Int) -> #(Int, Effect(Msg)) {
  #(0, island.every(milliseconds, Tick))
}
```

`island.subscription` takes a function that starts something, given the
function that sends the island a message, and returns the function that
stops it. Here `set_interval` and `clear_interval` are the app's own
JavaScript externals:

```gleam
island.subscription(fn(dispatch) {
  let id = set_interval(1000, fn() { dispatch(Tick) })
  fn() { clear_interval(id) }
})
```

If a removed island still receives messages seconds after it stopped, the
browser console says which island, so a forgotten interval is easy to
find. An island put back in the page starts again from its flags.

## Islands from packages

A package can ship islands for apps to use. Any package that depends on
`howdy_ui` can have a directory called `islands`, such as
`src/library/islands/rating.gleam`, and an app mounts those islands like
its own, with nothing to register:

```gleam
import library/islands/rating

island.mount(rating.island, with: 3)
```

The rule is the same everywhere: an island module is one in a directory
called `islands`. Mount a function from any other module and the island
does not start: the browser console says why, and in development so does
the page.

## Releases

A release has no Gleam compiler, so its islands are built and bundled as
it is exported. Export with Howdy UI rather than with
`gleam export erlang-shipment`:

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/ui export
```

It compiles the app, bundles every island it mounts into
`priv/howdy_islands` with [esbuild](https://esbuild.github.io), minified and
split into shared chunks with hashed names, then runs
`gleam export erlang-shipment`. A release serves the bundle with
`immutable` caching: a page of islands loads in a handful of requests.

esbuild is downloaded the first time, checked against a pinned checksum,
and kept in your user cache directory. Set `HOWDY_ESBUILD` to the path of
an esbuild of your own to use that instead.

If your build must run `gleam export erlang-shipment` itself, such as in a
Dockerfile, bundle just before it:

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/ui islands
gleam export erlang-shipment
```

### Stale bundles

The bundle records what it was built from: for every package with code in
it, the version and a fingerprint of each compiled module involved, Erlang
FFI included. A release checks it as it starts. If the code it runs is not
the code the bundle was built from, it refuses the bundle rather than
serving islands that no longer match the pages around them: it logs what
changed and the command to run, and the browser console says the same. The
islands then do not start, and the server-rendered HTML stays as it is.

A release holds no JavaScript sources, so it cannot see a change to a
JavaScript FFI file alone, in your app or a path dependency, made between
bundling and exporting. `gleam run -m howdy/ui export` does both in one
step, so nothing can change in between. Prefer it.

## Testing

An island's `init`, `update` and `view` are plain functions, so test them
directly, as you would a [live component's](/docs/ui/live-components#Testing).
To check what a page renders, render the island on the server:

```gleam
pub fn the_counter_starts_at_its_flags_test() {
  let html = island.mount(counter.island, with: 5) |> element.to_string
  assert string.contains(html, ">5<")
}
```

What happens in the browser needs a browser. `examples/islands` in the
Howdy repository drives Chromium against both `gleam run` and an exported
release.

## Limits

- Islands cannot use server-only code; see
  [What an island can use](#What-an-island-can-use).
- Mount the island's function, such as `counter.island`, not an anonymous
  function: the browser imports the module the function is in.
- Lustre cannot shut a browser app down, so an island removed from a live
  view leaves its runtime behind until the page reloads. Its update and
  view stop, and `every` and `subscription` stop their effects, so it does
  no more work.
- An island's flags are in the page's HTML. Do not put anything in them
  the visitor may not see.
